 succeeded in 0ms:
{
  "tactic": {
    "name": "Master the Seven Agentic Prompt Formats",
    "one_liner": "The prompt is the fundamental unit of engineering — so stop writing one-off prompts and start building a library of reusable, consistently-structured agentic prompts assembled from a fixed set of swappable Lego-block sections, scaled level by level from a one-line ad-hoc prompt to a prompt that writes and improves your other prompts.",
    "why_uncomfortable": "It asks engineers to treat 'prompt engineering' — the skill that got mocked as a joke — as one of the most important engineering disciplines of the age of agents, and to invest real design effort into files that look trivially small. It also demands a mindset most engineers have not made: you are no longer writing prompts only for yourself. Every prompt now has three readers — you, your team, and your agents (the stakeholder trifecta) — and a single well-crafted prompt can generate tens or hundreds of hours of work while a single bad one compounds failure at the same rate. That leverage forces you to slow down, standardize, and template work you used to fire off casually."
  },
  "mission": "Level up your agentic prompt engineering by mastering seven reusable prompt formats and the ~13 composable sections that make them up, so you can consistently CRUD (create/read/update/delete) reusable prompts at light speed for the trifecta. Being able to reach for the right level — usually Level 3 or 4 — and swap in exactly the sections you need is what lets you hand more and more work to compute, move from inside-the-loop to outside-the-loop to ZTE, and achieve asymmetric engineering leverage in the age of agents.",
  "stakeholder_trifecta": {
    "definition": "The three audiences every agentic prompt is written for at once: You, Your Team, and Your Agents. Named 'the stakeholder trifecta for the age of agents.' A new consumer of your work has appeared — your agent — and prompts must communicate clearly to all three.",
    "you": "Your future self returning to a prompt weeks later must instantly understand what it does. Consistent structure (collapse to H2s, read the top) makes that instant.",
    "team": "A coworker must be able to pick up the prompt cold and run it top to bottom. Dan's test: 'If I handed this to a coworker, could they complete this work top to bottom? If that's true, you've probably written a high quality prompt.'",
    "agents": "The most important reader. Powerful modern models reason over your prompt as a user prompt; consistent, information-dense sections reduce their confusion and let one prompt fire tens to hundreds of tool calls reliably for minutes to hours.",
    "dan_words": "\"We're now engineering for three audiences, you, your team, your agents. This is the stakeholder trifecta for the age of agents.\""
  },
  "core_concepts": [
    {
      "id": "prompt-is-fundamental-unit",
      "name": "The prompt is the fundamental unit of engineering",
      "plain_english": "In the age of agents the prompt — not the function, not the file — is the atomic unit of engineering work. Because every line of an agentic prompt can trigger tens to hundreds of tool calls and run for minutes to hours, prompt engineering (now agentic prompt engineering) becomes one of the highest-leverage skills an engineer can master.",
      "dan_words": "\"The prompt is now the fundamental unit of engineering. And with agents, every prompt you create becomes a force multiplier. One well-crafted prompt can generate tens and hundreds of hours of productive work. One bad prompt compounds failure at the same rate.\"",
      "misconception": "That prompt engineering is a soft, throwaway skill. Dan's rebuttal: it was 'once mocked as a joke,' but with agents it is now one of the most important skills for engineers to focus on and master."
    },
    {
      "id": "stakeholder-trifecta",
      "name": "The Stakeholder Trifecta (you / your team / your agents)",
      "plain_english": "You are not writing prompts only for yourself anymore. Three stakeholders read every prompt — you, your team, and your agents — and the winning move is a library of reusable, battle-tested prompts whose sections communicate clearly to all three. Consistency is what makes that communication cheap.",
      "dan_words": "\"What we're doing here is learning how to best communicate what we want to the trifecta, you, your team, and your agents. Most importantly, your agents.\"",
      "misconception": "That prompts are private scratch work. In the age of agents a prompt is shared infrastructure with a brand-new consumer — the agent — so it must be engineered like any other interface."
    },
    {
      "id": "composable-sections-lego-blocks",
      "name": "Composable sections (swappable Lego blocks)",
      "plain_english": "An agentic prompt is assembled from a fixed vocabulary of ~13 named sections (Title, Purpose, Variables, Workflow, Report, Metadata, Instructions, Relevant Files, Codebase Structure, Expertise, Template, Examples, High Level Prompt). Each has a distinct capability. You only add the sections you need and swap them in and out like Lego blocks — that composability plus consistency is what makes prompts reusable.",
      "dan_words": "\"The winners here are building libraries of reusable battle-tested agentic prompts with composable sections that work like lego blocks. You can swap these in and swap them out when and if you need them.\"",
      "misconception": "That you must use every section, or that section names are fixed law. You can name sections whatever you want; Dan uses 'the most consistent, the most information dense keywords.' The discipline is: only add what you need, and stay consistent."
    },
    {
      "id": "seven-levels",
      "name": "The Seven Levels of Agentic Prompt Formats",
      "plain_english": "Seven prompt formats that stack on each other, each unlocking one new capability: (1) High Level Prompt, (2) Workflow Prompt, (3) Control Flow Prompt, (4) Delegate Prompt, (5) Higher Order Prompt, (6) Template Metaprompt, (7) Self-Improving Prompt. Every level inherits the sections of the levels below it and adds one. Levels 1-4 cover 80-90% of real engineering work; levels 5-7 are 'meta' territory (prompts that build and improve prompts).",
      "dan_words": "\"All the prompts stack up on each other and the workflow prompt is an absolute pillar for all subsequent prompt levels... Levels one through let's say four will be 80 or 90% of all of your prompts.\"",
      "misconception": "That higher level = better. The right move is the lowest level that gets the job done — 'usually level three, level four is all you need.' You reach up only when the capability is required."
    },
    {
      "id": "workflow-is-the-pillar",
      "name": "The Workflow section is the pillar",
      "plain_english": "The Workflow section — a sequential, numbered play of exactly what you want the agent to do — is the single most useful section (graded S-tier usefulness) and the one that maps directly onto a modern agentic tool's todo/plan list. Control flow, loops, and delegation all live inside the workflow. It is the backbone of every level from 2 upward.",
      "dan_words": "\"Out of all the agentic prompt formats we'll look at, this is easily the most important one. Why is that? It's because of this workflow section... The most important section you'll write is the workflow.\"",
      "misconception": "That the flashy meta-prompt levels matter most. Dan says it over and over: 90% of the time all you need is a great workflow section at level three or four."
    },
    {
      "id": "input-workflow-output",
      "name": "Input → Workflow → Output framework",
      "plain_english": "A consistent three-step way to design any prompt: Inputs (Variables) → the actual Work (Workflow) → Output (Report). The input and output are especially for you and your team (what comes in, what goes out); the workflow is where the agent does the engineering. Use this frame to design prompts fast and predictably.",
      "dan_words": "\"It has the inputs, it has the workflow and it has the output, input, workflow, output. This is a very consistent three-step pattern you can use to think about how to design and build your prompts.\"",
      "misconception": "That every prompt needs all three. If you don't care about output format, drop Report; if nothing is passed in, drop Variables. The frame is a design lens, not a mandatory template."
    },
    {
      "id": "static-vs-dynamic-variables",
      "name": "Static vs dynamic variables",
      "plain_english": "Dynamic variables accept input at runtime via Claude Code's argument syntax ($1, $2, $ARGUMENTS) and change every run (e.g. USER_PROMPT: $1). Static variables are fixed values written into the prompt that you reference throughout and change in one place (e.g. PLAN_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY: `specs/`). Both use a consistent NAME: value syntax in a Variables section, referenced by name across the prompt so a single edit propagates everywhere.",
      "dan_words": "\"This is a static variable... a variable we're placing right in the prompt that doesn't change... versus a the user prompt, a dynamic variable, right? That changes as you pass in work... Notice how much time I'm saving by having just a single static variable here that we can reference throughout the prompt.\"",
      "misconception": "That variables need a special templating engine. It's natural-language convention — a consistent NAME: value syntax the model is powerful enough to resolve wherever it's referenced. (Dynamic variables come first, static second; prefer $1/$2 over $ARGUMENTS for multiple args.)"
    },
    {
      "id": "delegation-prompt-writes-prompts",
      "name": "Delegation — a prompt that writes prompts for sub-agents",
      "plain_english": "A Level-4 delegate prompt makes your primary agent the prompt engineer for its sub-agents: it designs detailed, self-contained prompts, defines clear output expectations, and fires N agents in parallel (Task tool) or a background CLI instance. Because LLMs are non-deterministic, spinning up multiple agents on different aspects of a problem returns diverse results. This is the 'D' (Delegate) of the R&D context-engineering framework from AH-1.",
      "dan_words": "\"Our primary agent is instructing the sub agents... Our primary agent is the prompt engineer for our sub agents. And this is where things get really tricky... we're writing prompts that write prompts.\"",
      "misconception": "That delegation is just 'more prompting.' It's structurally harder (S-tier usefulness, A-tier skill): you must consciously manage the flow of information between agents, because a stateless sub-agent only knows the self-contained prompt you hand it."
    },
    {
      "id": "template-metaprompt",
      "name": "Template Metaprompt — the prompt that builds prompts",
      "plain_english": "A Level-6 prompt whose Template section (a.k.a. Specified Format) encodes the exact output structure you want a NEW prompt to have, so the agent fills in the blanks and generates a well-formed prompt in your house style. Paired with a Documentation section (a context map the agent web-fetches first), it teaches your agent to build prompts like you and your team build them. This is the peak-leverage skill: once you can generate prompts, your agentic engineering velocity scales hard.",
      "dan_words": "\"This is the most powerful prompt you can write. It's the prompt that creates your other prompts... once you unlock this S tier skill and usefulness level, these template meta prompts at level six, your agentic engineering velocity scales up very, very quickly.\"",
      "misconception": "That meta-prompting is a novelty. Dan frames it as the highest-ROI investment in the codebase: 'this will pay you back massive dividends for every prompt you write that you can have your agent create for you.'"
    },
    {
      "id": "self-improving-prompt",
      "name": "Self-Improving Prompt — the agent that updates the agent",
      "plain_english": "A Level-7 (expert-level) prompt with a dynamic Expertise section that another prompt (or itself) updates with new knowledge over time. In the cc_hook_expert pattern, a dedicated _improve prompt reviews recent changes and rewrites ONLY the ## Expertise sections of the _plan and _build experts while leaving their Workflows stable — updating the context inside the prompt in a live way, so the expert grows more proficient in one narrow domain.",
      "dan_words": "\"We're having our agents update our agents. How? By having our prompts update our prompts... We're basically updating the context inside of our prompts in a live way. You have self-updating prompts.\"",
      "misconception": "That every prompt should self-improve. These are rare — 'it's unlikely that you even have a use case.' The pattern works best when a SEPARATE prompt is dedicated to updating the expertise, and only the Expertise section is touched (workflows stay stable)."
    },
    {
      "id": "system-vs-user-prompts",
      "name": "System prompts vs user prompts (scope & persistence)",
      "plain_english": "Every section graded in the tier list is for USER prompts — the prompts that flow into your context window (all your slash commands). System prompts are different: they set the rules for EVERY conversation, can't be changed mid-conversation, must handle many scenarios, and their mistakes scale to every user prompt. Written once, run many times. You append or overwrite them only when you build your own specialized agents (Claude Code SDK, AH-3). Best system-prompt sections: Purpose, Instructions, Examples (and a loose Workflow); avoid Variables, Report, Expertise, Templates, Metadata.",
      "dan_words": "\"The big idea here with a system prompt versus a user prompt is the scope and level of persistence... System prompts are rules for all conversations... A bad system prompt is bad instructions for the whole job.\"",
      "misconception": "That the tier-list grades apply to system prompts too. They don't — they're graded for user prompts. System prompts are one to three orders of magnitude more consequential and demand far more delicacy; most engineers rarely reach that level."
    },
    {
      "id": "consistency-beats-complexity",
      "name": "Consistency is king",
      "plain_english": "The through-line of the whole lesson: reusing the same prompt format and the same interchangeable sections every time is what makes you (and your team and your agents) faster. Consistency is the greatest weapon against confusion. Boredom is the goal — a repetitive, predictable prompt structure means you spend zero cycles re-learning your own tools and can CRUD prompts at light speed.",
      "dan_words": "\"Consistency is the greatest weapon against confusion for both you and your agent... consistency beats complexity, consistency makes you faster... Consistency is king when it comes to writing great agentic prompts.\"",
      "misconception": "That novelty or cleverness per prompt is good. Dan wants you 'annoyed and kind of bored of this workflow' — the sameness IS the feature; it's what lets one prompt (and ten more like it) be handed off without friction."
    }
  ],
  "seven_levels": [
