47:    <main class="chapter-main" id="main-content">
48:      <header class="hero">
49:        <span class="hero__chapter">Tactic #1 · Stop Coding</span>
51:        <p class="hero__dek">The decisive move is not a better code-generation prompt. It is turning a prompt into a reliable engineering workflow—and turning the agent into a component you can call.</p>
52:        <ul class="hero__meta" aria-label="Chapter details">
58:        <p class="hero__promise"><strong>Mastery promise.</strong> By the end, you will be able to diagnose agentic work with the Core Four, explain the exact difference between AI coding and agentic coding, reproduce Dan’s interactive and headless demos, and teach why “replace yourself” is a leverage strategy rather than a surrender.</p>
59:        <figure class="frame-card generated-plate generated-plate--hero">
60:          <img src="../media/generated/hero-agentic-systems.png" alt="Agentic Coding course plate showing one central agentic system coordinating code, workflows, tools, and several agents" width="1024" height="1536" fetchpriority="high">
61:          <figcaption>The chapter’s orientation: move from generating an isolated artifact to building systems that carry engineering work.</figcaption>
65:      <div class="mobile-toc">
66:        <details>
85:      <section class="chapter-section" id="orientation">
94:        <aside class="callout callout--tactic">
95:          <p class="callout__label">The chapter’s one-sentence thesis</p>
107:        <h3>How to use this chapter</h3>
108:        <p>Do not read it as a transcript recap. Each major idea repeats the same ten learning moves: failure, picture, one demo move, term, worked example, a small action, failure repair, current-practice overlay, retrieval, and teach-back. That repetition is intentional. Expertise is not the ability to recognize Dan’s phrases. It is the ability to retrieve the idea under pressure, operate it in the course repository, notice when it fails, and explain it without the chapter open.</p>
116:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-role">
170:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
215:          <aside class="current-practice">
225:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for the Agentic Engineer role">
226:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What behavior, not job title, defines Dan’s Agentic Engineer?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Building systems that can take actions as engineers can—such as branching, writing, running, committing, and reporting.</p></details></div>
227:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Why is an excellent one-shot code prompt still phase-one shaped?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Because the output is code while the human still carries the engineering workflow around it. Quality does not change who performs the transitions.</p></details></div>
228:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What is the smallest observable proof that your role has moved?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A system completes at least one meaningful multi-action chain without the human relaying intermediate results, then returns evidence for review.</p></details></div>
241:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-mission">
249:          <p>Suppose an engineer doubles their typing speed, memorizes every framework API, and uses autocomplete perfectly. They may produce more code per hour, but every additional unit still consumes their attention. Their throughput is coupled to presence. If they stop, the work stops.</p>
298:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
333:          <p>A boring five-minute workflow repeated 40 times may be a better leverage point than an impressive hour-long task performed once. This chapter does not authorize using any non-course repository in the lab; this ledger is a planning exercise for transfer after mastery.</p>
346:          <aside class="current-practice">
355:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for leverage and mission">
356:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Why does Dan say the “10x” number is arbitrary?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Because the multiplier comes from the leverage point you find. It could be 2x, 10x, or 100x; it does not come from a fixed amount of greater effort.</p></details></div>
357:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What makes the programmable demo leverage rather than just automation theater?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The workflow can be recreated repeatedly from a known state with low additional human attention, and it returns evidence through the report.</p></details></div>
358:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What does a commander of compute decide?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The target, context, constraints, bounded tools, success evidence, and timing of a system that performs engineering work.</p></details></div>
371:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-stop">
436:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
457:          <aside class="callout callout--why">
489:          <aside class="current-practice">
491:            <p>The locked overlay map pairs this lesson’s thesis with Dan’s public treatment, <em>AI Coding is NOT ENOUGH</em>. Use it after you can state the phase distinction yourself; the chapter’s course demo remains the graded evidence.</p>
506:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for Stop Coding">
507:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What exactly stops when you follow Tactic #1?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Your hand-typing of application code. Code generation and code understanding continue.</p></details></div>
508:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Where do the freed engineering cycles go?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Planning, reviewing, and creating closed-loop structures.</p></details></div>
509:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is fixing the last two lines by hand a course failure even when it is faster?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It completes the artifact while hiding the loop’s weakness. You lose the chance to improve agent instructions, feedback, and verification for repeatable future work.</p></details></div>
510:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What must increase when agents generate more code?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The quality and systematization of planning, review, validation, and governance.</p></details></div>
523:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-core-four">
578:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
645:          <aside class="current-practice">
654:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Core Four retrieval questions">
655:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Name the Big Three and the one added dimension.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context, model, prompt; tools are added to form the Core Four.</p></details></div>
656:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What exact capability makes the tools dimension phase-two significant?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Prompts that can reliably execute long chains of tool calls.</p></details></div>
657:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What is Dan’s rebuttal to “tool calling isn’t new”?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Novelty is not the deciding issue; the change is useful scale, performance, and reliable long-chain execution.</p></details></div>
658:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Which allowed tool proves the agent can commit but not publish?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>Bash(git commit:*)</code> is allowed, while no <code>git push</code> permission appears.</p></details></div>
671:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-ab">
674:        <p class="section-lead">The chapter’s A/B test holds repository, model, and operator constant. Prompt A creates a file. Prompt B carries an engineering workflow. Run both; the delta is the lesson.</p>
745:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
825:          <aside class="current-practice">
834:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="A/B demo retrieval questions">
835:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Name five actions Prompt B performed that Prompt A did not.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Branch checkout; second specified file; execution of both programs; Git add and commit; todo management and final report.</p></details></div>
836:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Why is <code>uv run</code> load-bearing in the comparison?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It turns generated code into observed behavior. The agent does not merely write; it executes and can use the outputs as evidence.</p></details></div>
837:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why does <code>programmable/prompt.md</code> create <code>main_aic.py</code> too?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>To make the headless workflow self-contained rather than dependent on the earlier interactive phase-one run.</p></details></div>
838:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What is the most useful definition of an Agentic Coding Prompt here?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A workflow specification containing a sequence of reliably executed tool calls.</p></details></div>
851:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-tool">
904:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
912:            <li>The <strong>architecture</strong> must present context and tools, preserve observations, manage turns, expose progress, handle errors, and stop at a useful boundary.</li>
925:              <tr><td>Builds and drains a todo checklist while retaining outputs</td><td>Agent architecture</td><td>The system externalizes progress and carries observations toward completion.</td></tr>
930:          <aside class="callout callout--warning">
932:            <p>Dan states that TAC will use millions of tokens across the full course and calls the investment “pay to play.” The chapter does not invent a dollar estimate; costs and plans change. The operational lesson is to budget enough model access that you can run the exercises rather than stopping at recognition.</p>
960:          <aside class="current-practice">
977:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Three essential elements retrieval questions">
978:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Name the three essential elements in order.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A powerful language model that can reason when needed; consistent long chains of tool calls; the right agent architecture.</p></details></div>
979:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Why can a strong coding model still be a weak agentic system?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It may not carry long tool chains reliably, or the surrounding architecture may fail to preserve state, route actions, handle errors, or stop well.</p></details></div>
980:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Which TAC-1 behavior most directly evidences architecture?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The todo plan, retained tool observations, bounded permissions, error/completion handling, and final report all evidence the surrounding agent architecture.</p></details></div>
981:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What is transferable when the UI and model names change?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The tactic, Core Four diagnosis, agentic prompt shape, and three-element evaluation checklist.</p></details></div>
994:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-programmable">
1065:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
1137:          <aside class="current-practice">
1155:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Programmable agent retrieval questions">
1156:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What single property makes Claude Code programmable in Dan’s demonstration?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It runs in the terminal, so any language with terminal access can invoke it through <code>claude -p</code>.</p></details></div>
1157:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What two additions make <code>prompt.md</code> more headless-ready than the README prompt?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It creates both runtime Python files, making the workflow self-contained, and it requires a REPORT with exact outputs.</p></details></div>
1158:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What host-side core do all three launchers share, and what does Shell omit?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>All read the prompt, invoke <code>claude -p</code>, and print returned text. The minimal Shell wrapper does not explicitly capture stderr or preserve the Claude process’s failing exit status after <code>echo</code>.</p></details></div>
1159:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Why is the “agent as function” analogy imperfect?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The call has side effects in the repository and terminal. Starting state, permissions, reports, and exit status therefore matter.</p></details></div>
1172:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-paradox">
1201:          <p>First Dan runs the agentic prompt interactively. He chooses it, pastes it, and watches the system carry the work. Then he deletes the branch and fires the same workflow through Python. The second run removes his interactive presence. By the time Dan states the contradicting truth near the close, the chapter has already enacted it.</p>
1203:          <aside class="callout callout--why">
1212:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
1260:          <aside class="current-practice">
1263:            <p class="current-practice__source">No additional video card here; the chapter stays within the locked maximum of three overlays.</p>
1269:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Replace yourself retrieval questions">
1270:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. State the contradicting truth in your own words.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Becoming harder to replace requires automating your own repeated execution and moving to higher-order ownership.</p></details></div>
1271:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What is replaced in the TAC-1 programmable move?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The human’s repeated prompt-pasting and carrying of the branch-write-run-commit-report workflow.</p></details></div>
1272:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What becomes more valuable after that replacement?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Judgment about goals, constraints, tool boundaries, evidence, risks, triggers, review, and improvement—plus ownership of the system.</p></details></div>
1273:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Why is “this launcher replaces an engineer” a bad interpretation?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It overstates a narrow workflow replacement. Credible progress names the exact repeated execution removed and the higher-order work that remains.</p></details></div>
1280:            <p><strong>Resolve the paradox in one minute.</strong> Use the interactive-to-programmable TAC-1 progression. Name what Dan replaces, what he still owns, and why the second kind of work creates more leverage.</p>
1286:      <section class="chapter-section chapter-section--wide" id="lab">
1291:        <aside class="callout callout--warning">
1305:        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-setup">
1341:        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-ab">
1393:        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-headless">
1444:        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-stretch">
1498:        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-troubleshooting">
1515:        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-final-pass">
1543:      <section class="chapter-section" id="mastery">
1546:        <p class="section-lead">Recognition is not the finish line. Close the chapter, answer on paper, then return to reveal. Score operations and reasoning, not exact prose.</p>
1549:        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative foundations quiz">
1550:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What are the Big Three?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context, model, prompt—present in AI coding whether or not they are visible in the interface.</p></details></div>
1551:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What upgrades them to the Core Four?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Tools, specifically prompts that can reliably execute long chains of tool calls.</p></details></div>
1552:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What does “Stop Coding” forbid, and what does it preserve?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It forbids hand-typing application code as the default instrument. It preserves and intensifies code generation, understanding, review, planning, and closed-loop design.</p></details></div>
1553:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Define Commander of Compute without saying “uses AI.”</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>An engineer who aims compute by specifying outcomes, context, constraints, bounded actions, evidence, and triggers to build systems that build systems.</p></details></div>
1557:        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative demo quiz">
1558:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">5. Reconstruct Prompt B’s state transitions without looking.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Checkout the demo branch; create <code>main_tac.py</code>; run <code>main_aic.py</code>; run <code>main_tac.py</code>; stage files; commit with the specified message; report completion.</p></details></div>
1559:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">6. Which runtime artifact must exist before the README Agentic Coding Prompt runs, and why?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>main_aic.py</code>, because Prompt B runs it but does not create it. The interactive A/B order supplies it.</p></details></div>
1560:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">7. What exact commit subject proves the Git portion completed?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>Demo agentic coding capabilities</code>.</p></details></div>
1561:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">8. What makes <code>prompt.md</code> self-contained and observable?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It creates both Python files, performs the branch/run/commit chain, and demands exact program outputs in REPORT.</p></details></div>
1565:        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative diagnosis quiz">
1566:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">9. The prompt says commit; the model plans commit; the agent cannot execute it after S3. Which dimension failed?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Tools/permission: the commit tool pattern was removed from the allowlist.</p></details></div>
1567:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">10. The agent edits the wrong <code>prompt.md</code> in a larger repo. What two dimensions should you probe first?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context—what paths and repository content it saw—and prompt—whether the target path was explicit.</p></details></div>
1568:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">11. A tool calls all commands but forgets the first program’s output before REPORT. Which essential element is most suspect?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The agent architecture’s state/observation management, though the combined system should be evaluated rather than blaming a label alone.</p></details></div>
1569:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">12. Why is repeated rerolling a weak diagnostic method?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It changes stochastic output without isolating whether context, model, prompt, or tools caused the failed state transition.</p></details></div>
1573:        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative transfer quiz">
1574:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">13. A prompt generates five excellent files, but a human runs tests, commits, and reports. AI coding or agentic coding?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Primarily AI coding. Output quantity does not move the workflow boundary; the human still carries the engineering transitions.</p></details></div>
1575:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">14. Why is exact report-back a seed of closed-loop engineering?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It turns internal work into an externally checkable claim that a human or another program can compare with reality and use for the next decision.</p></details></div>
1576:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">15. When is a tiny workflow a better leverage point than a huge one?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>When it repeats often, has clear state transitions and evidence, and can be carried reliably with low marginal human attention.</p></details></div>
1577:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">16. Resolve “replace yourself” in one sentence.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Automate your repeated execution so your scarce attention moves to judgment, system ownership, and the next leverage point.</p></details></div>
1580:        <aside class="callout callout--why">
1599:      <section class="chapter-section" id="glossary">
