+  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
+  <meta name="description" content="A teaching-first companion to Agentic Horizon lesson 2: master seven stackable agentic prompt formats, thirteen composable sections, prompt scope, and a course-grounded lab.">
+  <title>AH-2 · Seven Levels Agentic Prompt Formats — IndyDevDan Expert Book</title>
+  <link rel="stylesheet" href="../design/tokens.css">
+  <link rel="stylesheet" href="../design/base.css">
+  <link rel="stylesheet" href="../design/components.css">
+  <link rel="stylesheet" href="10-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats.assets.css">
+</head>
+<body>
+  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to chapter content</a>
+  <div class="reading-progress" aria-hidden="true"><span class="reading-progress__bar" id="top-progress"></span></div>
+  <header class="site-bar">
+    <a class="site-bar__home" href="../index.html" aria-label="Back to the book hub">← IndyDevDan Expert Book</a>
+    <span class="site-bar__meta">VOL II · AH · LESSON 10</span>
+  </header>
+
+  <div class="chapter-shell">
+    <aside class="chapter-rail" aria-label="Chapter navigation">
+      <p class="rail-kicker">Volume II · Agentic Horizon</p>
+      <h2>Seven Levels Agentic Prompt Formats</h2>
+      <div class="rail-progress">
+        <p class="micro-label">Reading progress</p>
+        <div class="rail-progress__line"><span class="rail-progress__fill" id="rail-progress"></span></div>
+        <p class="rail-progress__copy"><span id="progress-label">0%</span><span>of chapter</span></p>
+      </div>
+      <nav class="chapter-nav" aria-label="On this page">
+        <ol>
+          <li><a href="#orientation"><span class="chapter-nav__n">00</span><span>Orientation</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#infrastructure"><span class="chapter-nav__n">01</span><span>Prompt as infrastructure</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#composable"><span class="chapter-nav__n">02</span><span>Composable sections</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#levels-one-two"><span class="chapter-nav__n">03</span><span>Levels 1–2</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#level-three"><span class="chapter-nav__n">04</span><span>Level 3 · control flow</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#level-four"><span class="chapter-nav__n">05</span><span>Level 4 · delegation</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#levels-five-six"><span class="chapter-nav__n">06</span><span>Levels 5–6 · meta</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#level-seven"><span class="chapter-nav__n">07</span><span>Level 7 · improve</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#tier-list"><span class="chapter-nav__n">08</span><span>Tier-list choice map</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#scope"><span class="chapter-nav__n">09</span><span>User vs system</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#current-practice"><span class="chapter-nav__n">10</span><span>Current practice</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#lab"><span class="chapter-nav__n">11</span><span>Graded lab</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#mastery"><span class="chapter-nav__n">12</span><span>Cumulative mastery</span></a></li>
+          <li><a href="#glossary"><span class="chapter-nav__n">13</span><span>Glossary</span></a></li>
+        </ol>
+      </nav>
+    </aside>
+
+    <main class="chapter-main" id="main-content">
+      <header class="hero hero--prompts">
+        <span class="hero__chapter">Agentic Horizon · Lesson 10 · Master the seven formats</span>
+        <h1>Seven Levels Agentic Prompt Formats</h1>
+        <p class="hero__dek">Stop treating prompts as disposable chat. Build them as a small, consistent language of reusable sections—and climb only as high as the work requires.</p>
+        <ul class="hero__meta" aria-label="Chapter details">
+          <li>Video spine · 57:21</li>
+          <li>Core lab · 60–90 min</li>
+          <li>Course repo · agentic-horizon/agentic-prompt-engineering</li>
+          <li>12 verified source frames</li>
+        </ul>
+        <p class="hero__promise"><strong>Mastery promise.</strong> By the end, you will be able to name the seven formats and the capability each adds, assemble prompts from thirteen sections without cargo-culting them, choose the lowest useful level, distinguish workflow from instructions and user prompts from system prompts, and prove your understanding against the lesson’s actual command files.</p>
+
+        <div class="hero-stack" role="img" aria-label="Seven prompt levels rising from a high level prompt to a self-improving prompt">
+          <div class="hero-stack__level" style="--i:1"><b>1</b><span>High level</span></div>
+          <div class="hero-stack__level" style="--i:2"><b>2</b><span>Workflow</span></div>
+          <div class="hero-stack__level" style="--i:3"><b>3</b><span>Control flow</span></div>
+          <div class="hero-stack__level" style="--i:4"><b>4</b><span>Delegate</span></div>
+          <div class="hero-stack__level" style="--i:5"><b>5</b><span>Higher order</span></div>
+          <div class="hero-stack__level" style="--i:6"><b>6</b><span>Template meta</span></div>
+          <div class="hero-stack__level" style="--i:7"><b>7</b><span>Self-improving</span></div>
+        </div>
+      </header>
+
+      <div class="mobile-toc">
+        <details>
+          <summary>Chapter contents</summary>
+          <ol>
+            <li><a href="#orientation">Orientation</a></li><li><a href="#infrastructure">Prompt as infrastructure</a></li><li><a href="#composable">Composable sections</a></li><li><a href="#levels-one-two">Levels 1–2</a></li><li><a href="#level-three">Level 3</a></li><li><a href="#level-four">Level 4</a></li><li><a href="#levels-five-six">Levels 5–6</a></li><li><a href="#level-seven">Level 7</a></li><li><a href="#tier-list">Tier list</a></li><li><a href="#scope">User vs system</a></li><li><a href="#current-practice">Current practice</a></li><li><a href="#lab">Graded lab</a></li><li><a href="#mastery">Mastery</a></li><li><a href="#glossary">Glossary</a></li>
+          </ol>
+        </details>
+      </div>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="orientation">
+        <span class="section-number">00</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">The staircase is a capability map, not a status ladder</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The seductive mistake is to hear “seven levels” and aim immediately for seven. This lesson teaches the opposite reflex: identify the capability the work needs, then use the lowest level that supplies it.</p>
+
+        <p>A prompt can be only a title and a reusable request. It can also be a miniature program: accept variables, execute a numbered workflow, branch, loop, start other agents, consume a plan, generate a new prompt, or update its own domain knowledge. Those are seven distinct capabilities. They stack, but the stack does not imply that the top belongs everywhere.</p>
+        <p>Dan’s practical center of gravity is much lower. Levels one through four cover roughly 80–90% of the work described in the lesson. A clear workflow, perhaps with an early return or a carefully scoped delegation, is usually enough. Levels five through seven matter because they let prompts operate on other prompts and on their own knowledge. They are powerful precisely because they are specialized, not because they should be the default.</p>
+
+        <div class="level-staircase" role="img" aria-label="Seven prompt formats, each adding one capability and showing usefulness and skill grades">
+          <div class="level-step" style="--i:1;--level-color:var(--level-1)"><span class="level-step__n">01</span><strong>High Level Prompt</strong><p>Save a reusable ad-hoc request.</p><span class="level-step__grade">B / D</span></div>
+          <div class="level-step" style="--i:2;--level-color:var(--level-2)"><span class="level-step__n">02</span><strong>Workflow Prompt</strong><p>Add an ordered input → work → output play.</p><span class="level-step__grade">A / C</span></div>
+          <div class="level-step" style="--i:3;--level-color:var(--level-3)"><span class="level-step__n">03</span><strong>Control Flow Prompt</strong><p>Add conditions, loops, and early returns.</p><span class="level-step__grade">A / B</span></div>
+          <div class="level-step" style="--i:4;--level-color:var(--level-4)"><span class="level-step__n">04</span><strong>Delegate Prompt</strong><p>Add other agents and explicit information flow.</p><span class="level-step__grade">S / A</span></div>
+          <div class="level-step" style="--i:5;--level-color:var(--level-5)"><span class="level-step__n">05</span><strong>Higher Order Prompt</strong><p>Accept another prompt, plan, or bundle as input.</p><span class="level-step__grade">B / A</span></div>
+          <div class="level-step" style="--i:6;--level-color:var(--level-6)"><span class="level-step__n">06</span><strong>Template Metaprompt</strong><p>Generate a new prompt in a specified format.</p><span class="level-step__grade">S / S</span></div>
+          <div class="level-step" style="--i:7;--level-color:var(--level-7)"><span class="level-step__n">07</span><strong>Self-Improving Prompt</strong><p>Update domain Expertise while keeping the procedure stable.</p><span class="level-step__grade">A / S</span></div>
+        </div>
+
+        <aside class="callout callout--warning">
+          <p class="callout__label">Read the grades correctly</p>
+          <p>Each pair is <strong>usefulness / skill</strong>, with S highest and D lowest. The Level-2 <em>format</em> is A/C, while the <code>## Workflow</code> <em>section inside it</em> is S/C. Confusing those two objects produces a wrong tier list.</p>
+        </aside>
+
+        <h3>What you are training</h3>
+        <ul class="learning-objectives">
+          <li><strong>Classification</strong>Name the one capability that moves a prompt from each level to the next.</li>
+          <li><strong>Composition</strong>Select only the sections a task needs from the canonical set of thirteen.</li>
+          <li><strong>Construction</strong>Write an executable Workflow with visible variables, control flow, and report evidence.</li>
+          <li><strong>Judgment</strong>Choose Level 3 or 4 when it is sufficient instead of adding meta-complexity for its own sake.</li>
+          <li><strong>Scope</strong>Explain why the tier list grades user prompts and why system prompts demand a different design.</li>
+          <li><strong>Transfer</strong>Use the course commands as worked specimens, then improve a copied command without leaving the course codebase.</li>
+        </ul>
+
+        <p class="source-note"><strong>Source discipline.</strong> This chapter treats the shipped lesson code as the operational source and the final on-screen grid as the grading source. The app contains thirteen section entities in <code>apps/prompt_tier_list/src/constants/entities.ts</code>; older prose that says twelve is documentation drift. The lesson’s model IDs and specific MCP dependencies are preserved as period details, not silently modernized.</p>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="infrastructure">
+        <span class="section-number">01</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">A prompt is infrastructure for three readers</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The lesson’s reversal is not “write prettier chat messages.” It is that an agentic prompt can aim a long chain of tool use, so the prompt becomes shared engineering infrastructure whose quality compounds.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — the leverage reversal</p>
+          <h3>One casual line can become hundreds of consequential actions</h3>
+          <p>A one-off chat reply disappears. An agentic prompt can run for minutes or hours, call tools tens or hundreds of times, write files, launch agents, and hand results into the next workflow. The prompt sits upstream of all those transitions. Ambiguity therefore does not stay local: it is repeated by compute. Clarity compounds in the same direction.</p>
+          <p>That is why Dan names the prompt—not the function or file—the fundamental unit of engineering in this operating model. The claim is about leverage. The function is still code; the file is still a repository artifact. But the prompt is the unit that can mobilize an agent across many functions and files.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the three readers</p>
+          <h3>You, your team, your agents</h3>
+          <div class="audience-grid" aria-label="Stakeholder trifecta responsibilities">
+            <article><strong>You</strong><p>Your future self should collapse the headings, scan the top, and recover the prompt’s purpose without reconstructing yesterday’s context.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>Your team</strong><p>A coworker should be able to execute the play top to bottom, understand its inputs, and know what evidence completion produces.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>Your agents</strong><p>The newest and most important executor must be able to turn the Workflow into a plan and act without guessing at hidden intent.</p></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story — the lesson’s opening frame</p>
+          <h3>The audience changed before the format did</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t0052-stakeholder-trifecta.jpg" alt="Slide titled The Stakeholder Trifecta connecting You, Your Agents, and Your Team" width="1920" height="1080" fetchpriority="high">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">00:52</span>The chapter starts with audience, not syntax. Consistency matters because the same prompt must remain legible to its author, transferable to a teammate, and executable by an agent.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p>Imagine opening <code>prime.md</code> three months after writing it. As its author, you recognize a small Purpose, a two-step Workflow, and a Report. A teammate sees the exact repository observations to collect. The agent sees ordered work it can map into its todo list. One artifact serves all three because its structure makes hidden assumptions visible.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p>
+          <h3>The Stakeholder Trifecta</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p><p><strong>The Stakeholder Trifecta</strong> is you, your team, and your agents. Agentic prompt engineering is the discipline of communicating intended work clearly to all three, most importantly the agents that execute it.</p></aside>
+          <p>This definition changes the quality test. “The model understood me once” is too weak. A reusable prompt should survive time, another human reader, and another agent run. That makes the prompt closer to an interface than a sticky note.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — repair a private prompt</p>
+          <h3>From a memory cue to a reusable interface</h3>
+          <div class="comparison">
+            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Private scratch</h3><p>Useful only while the missing context is still in your head.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><pre><code>check the repo and tell me what matters</code></pre></div></article>
+            <article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Shared prompt</h3><p>Names the observations and the report contract.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><pre><code># Prime
+
+## Workflow
+1. Run `git ls-files`.
+2. Read `README.md`.
+
+## Report
+- Summarize the codebase.</code></pre></div></article>
+          </div>
+          <p>The second prompt is not superior because it is longer. It is superior because the work boundary is inspectable. The author can revise one step. A teammate can predict what it will touch. The agent can execute without inventing the meaning of “what matters.”</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — three-reader review</p>
+          <h3>Interrogate one prompt from each seat</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Open <code>.claude/commands/prime.md</code> in the course repository.</li>
+            <li>As future-you, underline the minimum text needed to recognize its job.</li>
+            <li>As a teammate, circle every input, action, and expected output that removes a guess.</li>
+            <li>As the agent, number the executable state transitions.</li>
+            <li>Write one sentence about what would become ambiguous if the <code>## Workflow</code> heading disappeared.</li>
+          </ol>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: equating reuse with saving text</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> a vague sentence is copied into a command file, but every run still begins with clarification and every teammate interprets it differently.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> make the contract visible. Name the purpose, turn the work into ordered steps, expose changing inputs as variables, and specify a report only when its shape matters. Reuse begins when the missing context stops living only in the author.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice — durable idea, newer package</p>
+          <h3>The file container can change; the three readers remain</h3>
+          <p>The course demonstrates reusable behavior as slash-command Markdown files. Current agent tooling also packages reusable engineering as Agent Skills. That changes discovery and distribution, not the chapter’s core design question: can you, a teammate, and an agent recover the purpose, procedure, and evidence?</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval — close the page first</p>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name the three stakeholders and identify the newest executor.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>You, your team, and your agents; the agent is the newest and most important executor of the prompt.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why can a bad agentic prompt compound failure?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Because it sits upstream of a long execution chain. Its ambiguity can be repeated across many tool calls, files, minutes, and downstream workflows.</p></details></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p>
+          <div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Teach this in sixty seconds:</strong> explain why “the prompt is the fundamental unit of engineering” is a leverage claim, not a claim that functions or files vanished. Then describe how one well-structured <code>prime.md</code> serves all three stakeholders.</p></div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="composable">
+        <span class="section-number">02</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Build prompts from thirteen swappable sections</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Consistency does not mean every prompt has the same length. It means every capability has a familiar home, and absent capabilities leave no empty ceremony behind.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — the wall-of-text problem</p>
+          <h3>A prompt you cannot scan is a prompt you cannot maintain</h3>
+          <p>A long prompt without stable section names forces every reader to rediscover its grammar. Where do inputs enter? Which bullets are executable? Which files are context rather than targets? Is the last paragraph a report contract or another instruction? The model may recover the shape, but the trifecta pays that interpretation cost on every read.</p>
+          <p>The lesson replaces bespoke prose with a small vocabulary of named sections. Think of them as Lego blocks: each block contributes a capability, and a prompt contains only the blocks its job needs.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — collapse to the headings</p>
+          <h3>See the prompt as a stack before reading its sentences</h3>
+          <div class="section-grid" aria-label="Thirteen composable prompt sections">
+            <article><strong># Title</strong><p>Action-oriented name.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>## High Level Prompt</strong><p>Detail-light core request.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>## Metadata</strong><p>Frontmatter for tools, arguments, description, model.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>## Purpose</strong><p>Direct statement of intent.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>## Variables</strong><p>Dynamic inputs and static values.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>## Instructions</strong><p>Auxiliary behavioral guardrails.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>## Relevant Files</strong><p>Specific files the agent should read.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>## Codebase Structure</strong><p>A map for locating context quickly.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>## Workflow</strong><p>The ordered executable play.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>## Expertise</strong><p>Embedded domain knowledge.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>## Template</strong><p>Exact structure to generate.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>## Examples</strong><p>Concrete demonstrations of desired behavior.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>## Report</strong><p>Output and completion evidence.</p></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story — quick-plan becomes blocks</p>
+          <h3>The headings tell the story before the details do</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t0765-quick-plan-collapsed-sections.jpg" alt="Editor showing quick-plan.md collapsed to Variables, Instructions, Workflow, and Report headings" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">12:45</span>With its H2 sections collapsed, <code>quick-plan.md</code> becomes an architectural outline: inputs, surrounding guardrails, the work, and the response. The pinned course-era model ID is a metadata detail, not part of the timeless structure.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p>This view is a maintenance tool. You can compare two prompts by their headings before comparing prose. You can notice that one prompt has a Report and another intentionally does not. You can find a Variables block without searching for every <code>$1</code>. You can review whether knowledge accidentally leaked into the Workflow or whether execution steps are hiding inside Instructions.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p>
+          <h3>Composable sections</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Working definition</p><p><strong>Composable sections</strong> are consistent, information-dense prompt regions that each carry a recognizable capability. Add the blocks the task requires; omit those it does not. The discipline is composability plus consistency—not completeness.</p></aside>
+          <p>The names are a house style, not a protocol. Dan explicitly allows other names. The value comes from reusing names that the trifecta can recognize immediately. If every prompt calls the same concept something different—“steps,” “process,” “procedure,” “do this,” “execution”—you keep spending attention on translation.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — four sections, four jobs</p>
+          <h3>Read <code>quick-plan.md</code> as a composition</h3>
+          <div class="choice-grid">
+            <article><strong>Variables</strong><p><code>USER_PROMPT: $ARGUMENTS</code> changes each run. <code>PLAN_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY: `specs/`</code> is a fixed value referenced throughout.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>Instructions</strong><p>Auxiliary planning rules surround the play. They support the workflow but are not the ordered state transitions.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>Workflow + Report</strong><p>The Workflow performs the planning; Report defines what the agent returns after the plan is written.</p></article>
+          </div>
+          <p>Now remove a capability. If a prompt accepts no runtime input, omit Variables. If the generated artifact itself is the only needed output and no response shape matters, omit Report. <code>edit_image.md</code> demonstrates that omission: it retains loops and conditions but no Report. A missing section can be an intentional design decision.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — minimum viable composition</p>
+          <h3>Select sections for three task cards</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li><strong>List the agent’s tools, no arguments, plain response.</strong> Choose Title plus High Level Prompt.</li>
+            <li><strong>Read a supplied file, run two checks, return JSON.</strong> Choose Purpose, Variables, Workflow, and Report; add Metadata only if tool or argument declaration matters.</li>
+            <li><strong>Give an agent a repository map so it can locate targets quickly.</strong> Choose Codebase Structure; do not label the whole tree Relevant Files unless the agent should read every entry.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <p>For each card, cross out one tempting section you do not need and state why its capability is absent.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Two pairs weak learners collapse into one</h3>
+          <div class="confusion-grid">
+            <article><strong>Workflow ≠ Instructions</strong><p><strong>Fail:</strong> put the main play in an undifferentiated list of advice. <strong>Fix:</strong> Workflow is the numbered sequence the agent executes; Instructions are auxiliary rules around those steps and can often be nested into the relevant step.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>Codebase Structure ≠ Relevant Files</strong><p><strong>Fail:</strong> tell the agent to read an entire tree merely because it is shown. <strong>Fix:</strong> Codebase Structure is a location map; Relevant Files is the smaller set the agent should actually open.</p></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice — progressive disclosure</p>
+          <h3>Keep the reusable core scannable</h3>
+          <p>Modern skill packaging can load instructions and references progressively, but the same editorial test applies inside the core prompt: can a reader see its sections at a glance, and does each section carry only its own job? Packaging can reduce context cost; it cannot rescue a confused internal structure.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval — distinguish, do not recite</p>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Do you need all thirteen sections? Give two examples of intentional omission.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>No. Omit Variables when no values enter at runtime; omit Report when no response structure matters. The task determines the composition.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">A tree shows the repository layout but should not be read wholesale. Which section owns it?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Codebase Structure. Relevant Files names specific files the agent is expected to read.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is the difference between Workflow and Instructions?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Workflow is the ordered executable play. Instructions are auxiliary guardrails that support the play and can often live under the step they constrain.</p></details></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p>
+          <div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Use a physical-block metaphor:</strong> teach a partner why thirteen available blocks do not imply thirteen required blocks. Include both confusing pairs, then explain how collapsed headings make prompt maintenance cheaper for the trifecta.</p></div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="levels-one-two">
+        <span class="section-number">03</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Start reusable; then make the work executable</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Level 1 captures a repeated request. Level 2 adds the chapter’s pillar: a Workflow whose sequence is legible to the trifecta and executable by an agent.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — three times marks a pattern</p>
+          <h3>Do not wait for the perfect automation candidate</h3>
+          <p>If you have successfully asked an agent to do the same kind of work several times, repetition is already telling you something. The wording, context, and output expectations should stop living in chat history. Level 1 is the smallest move that recognizes the pattern: put the request in a named file so it can be reused.</p>
+          <p>But saved text is only a starting point. As soon as the work has multiple observable transitions, Level 2 makes the sequence explicit. Dan calls the Workflow Prompt the largest milestone and the pillar of every later level. It converts “do this” into a play.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — two levels, one capability gap</p>
+          <h3>From a reusable request to input → work → output</h3>
+          <div class="flow-lens" role="img" aria-label="Variables flow into Workflow which flows into Report">
+            <div class="flow-lens__block"><b>Input</b><code>## Variables</code><p>What changes per run, plus fixed values worth naming once.</p></div>
+            <div class="flow-lens__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
+            <div class="flow-lens__block"><b>Work</b><code>## Workflow</code><p>The ordered actions the agent carries from start to finish.</p></div>
+            <div class="flow-lens__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
+            <div class="flow-lens__block"><b>Output</b><code>## Report</code><p>The response structure and evidence completion should expose.</p></div>
+          </div>
+          <p>This is a design lens, not a mandatory form. A prompt with no runtime input can omit Variables. A prompt whose artifact is self-evident can omit Report. Workflow is the capability that defines Level 2.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story — the smallest prompt, then the pillar</p>
+          <h3>Two source frames show the exact transition</h3>
+          <div class="frame-sequence">
+            <figure class="frame-card">
+              <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t0250-l1-all-tools-prompt.jpg" alt="Editor showing all_tools.md with a title and one high-level request" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">04:10</span><code>all_tools.md</code> is a complete Level-1 prompt: a clear title and a reusable request. Its small size is the point—saving the pattern requires almost no ceremony.</figcaption>
+            </figure>
+            <figure class="frame-card">
+              <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t0456-l2-prime-workflow-report.jpg" alt="Editor showing prime.md with Purpose, Workflow, and Report sections" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">07:36</span><code>prime.md</code> adds the Level-2 capability: an ordered Workflow—list tracked files, read the README—and a Report that turns observations into a concise codebase summary.</figcaption>
+            </figure>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p>
+          <h3>Level 1: High Level Prompt · Level 2: Workflow Prompt</h3>
+          <div class="format-strip" aria-label="Seven prompt formats with grades">
+            <article style="--level-color:var(--level-1)"><b>01</b><strong>High Level</strong><p>Reusable request · B usefulness / D skill.</p></article>
+            <article style="--level-color:var(--level-2)"><b>02</b><strong>Workflow</strong><p>Ordered play · A usefulness / C skill.</p></article>
+            <article style="--level-color:var(--level-3)"><b>03</b><strong>Control Flow</strong><p>Branch and loop · A/B.</p></article>
+            <article style="--level-color:var(--level-4)"><b>04</b><strong>Delegate</strong><p>Other agents · S/A.</p></article>
+            <article style="--level-color:var(--level-5)"><b>05</b><strong>Higher Order</strong><p>Prompt as input · B/A.</p></article>
+            <article style="--level-color:var(--level-6)"><b>06</b><strong>Template Meta</strong><p>Prompt as output · S/S.</p></article>
+            <article style="--level-color:var(--level-7)"><b>07</b><strong>Self-Improving</strong><p>Knowledge updates · A/S.</p></article>
+          </div>
+          <p>A Level-1 prompt generally uses one to three sections: Title, the required High Level Prompt, perhaps Purpose. A Level-2 prompt requires Workflow and can add Variables, Instructions, Report, Metadata, Relevant Files, or Codebase Structure as its job demands. The format grade of Level 2 is A/C; its defining <code>## Workflow</code> section is S/C.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — read the real variables</p>
+          <h3><code>quick-plan.md</code> separates what moves from what stays</h3>
+          <div class="prompt-snippet">
+            <pre><code>## Variables
+
+USER_PROMPT: $ARGUMENTS
+PLAN_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY: specs/
+
+## Workflow
+
+- Understand USER_PROMPT.
+- Create the plan in PLAN_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY.
+
+## Report
+
+- Report the path to the saved plan.</code></pre>
+            <p class="prompt-snippet__note"><strong>Dynamic:</strong> <code>USER_PROMPT</code> changes with each invocation. <strong>Static:</strong> <code>PLAN_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY</code> stays fixed until the prompt author changes it once.</p>
+          </div>
+          <p>Static does not mean immutable. It means the value is written into the prompt rather than supplied by the caller. Naming it once creates a maintenance seam. Dynamic variables belong first, followed by static values, so readers see the call interface before the internal configuration.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — promote a repeated request</p>
+          <h3>Write the smallest Level 2 that can work</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Choose a repeated course-repository task, such as finding all TODO comments.</li>
+            <li>Write one Level-1 sentence that describes the outcome.</li>
+            <li>Add a dynamic search-root variable only if callers should change the root.</li>
+            <li>Turn the actual work into three to five numbered Workflow steps.</li>
+            <li>Add a Report only if the response needs a particular structure or evidence.</li>
+            <li>Collapse the headings. If the architecture is still recognizable, the composition is doing its job.</li>
+          </ol>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: jumping to “meta” before mastering sequence</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> the prompt contains templates, expertise, and elaborate metadata, yet its actual work remains a vague paragraph.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> delete the decorative complexity and repair the Workflow. Can a coworker perform each step top to bottom? Can the agent see when one step ends and the next begins? Does the Report prove the consequential transitions? The everyday bargain is not the fanciest format; it is the Workflow section—S usefulness at C skill.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice — treat metadata as replaceable</p>
+          <h3>The procedure should outlive a pinned model</h3>
+          <p>The course’s <code>quick-plan.md</code> metadata pins <code>claude-opus-4-1-20250805</code>. Preserve that as a historical implementation detail when studying the file. The lesson’s durable architecture is that Metadata contains tool, model, description, and argument declarations so they can change without rewriting the Workflow.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval — reconstruct the jump</p>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What single capability defines Level 2?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>An explicit sequential Workflow. Variables, Report, and other sections are optional supporting blocks.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Give the real dynamic and static variables from <code>quick-plan.md</code>.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>USER_PROMPT: $ARGUMENTS</code> is dynamic. <code>PLAN_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY: specs/</code> is static.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why are Level-2 Workflow Prompt and <code>## Workflow</code> graded differently?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>They are different graded entities. The whole format is A usefulness/C skill; the Workflow section itself is S usefulness/C skill.</p></details></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p>
+          <div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Draw, then explain:</strong> sketch Input → Workflow → Output. Explain why it is a lens rather than a mandatory three-block template, then show exactly how <code>all_tools.md</code> becomes conceptually different from <code>prime.md</code>.</p></div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="level-three">
+        <span class="section-number">04</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Level 3 makes decisions visible</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">A workflow becomes control flow when it can stop, branch, or repeat. The new capability lives inside <code>## Workflow</code>; it does not require a separate “logic” section.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — the dangerous happy path</p>
+          <h3>What should the agent do when a prerequisite is missing?</h3>
+          <p>A linear prompt often assumes its input exists, its tool is available, and every repeated item can be processed identically. When one assumption fails, the agent fills the gap. It guesses a path, improvises a tool, skips an item, or continues from partial state. Level 3 replaces those invisible decisions with explicit branches.</p>
+          <p>The goal is not programming-language syntax. Natural language is enough if the boundaries are visible: “If no path is supplied, stop.” “For each requested image, repeat this block.” “If the prerequisite tool is absent, report and exit.”</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — branch and loop</p>
+          <h3>Two shapes inside one Workflow</h3>
+          <div class="workflow" aria-label="Control flow with an early return and a repeated block">
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Receive input</strong><code>PATH_TO_PLAN</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Guard</strong><code>missing? STOP</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Read plan</strong><code>PATH_TO_PLAN</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Repeat</strong><code>&lt;work-loop&gt;</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Report</strong><code>git diff --stat</code></div>
+          </div>
+          <p>The guard shortens the path when the prompt cannot proceed safely. The loop makes repetition explicit. Both are reasoning about sequence, so both belong inside Workflow.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story — line 17 stops the run</p>
+          <h3>The course supplies a branch you can trigger on purpose</h3>
+          <div class="frame-sequence">
+            <figure class="frame-card">
+              <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t1245-l3-build-line17-control-flow.jpg" alt="Editor showing build.md line 17 with a STOP guard when PATH_TO_PLAN is absent" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">20:45</span>Line 17 is the clearest Level-3 specimen: “If no <code>PATH_TO_PLAN</code> is provided, STOP immediately and ask the user.” Missing input does not become guessed input.</figcaption>
+            </figure>
+            <figure class="frame-card">
+              <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t1450-l3-create-image-loop.jpg" alt="Editor showing create_image.md prerequisite checks and an image-loop XML block" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">24:10</span><code>create_image.md</code> shows the second control-flow shape: prerequisite STOP checks followed by a visibly delimited <code>&lt;image-loop&gt;</code> repeated <code>NUMBER_OF_IMAGES</code> times.</figcaption>
+            </figure>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p>
+          <h3>Level 3: Control Flow Prompt · A usefulness / B skill</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Capability added</p><p><strong>Control flow</strong> is conditions, loops, and early returns embedded in a Workflow. It raises the skill cost because the author must reason about alternate paths and termination, not only an ideal sequence.</p></aside>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — one file, three levels</p>
+          <h3><code>build.md</code> is a stacked specimen</h3>
+          <div class="proof-grid">
+            <article><strong>Level 2</strong><p>It has a Workflow: validate input, read a plan, implement it, then report changed files.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>Level 3</strong><p>Line 17 introduces an early return when <code>PATH_TO_PLAN</code> is absent.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>Level 5</strong><p>The supplied plan file is itself a reusable prompt-like artifact accepted as input by the stable build wrapper.</p></article>
+          </div>
+          <p>Formats stack, so classification can be plural. Calling <code>build.md</code> Level 5 does not erase its Workflow or early return. The highest capability present explains what else it can do; the lower capabilities remain operational.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — add one safe branch</p>
+          <h3>Promote a copy of <code>prime.md</code></h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Copy <code>.claude/commands/prime.md</code> inside the course repository.</li>
+            <li>Inside Workflow, add: if <code>README.md</code> is missing, stop and report the missing prerequisite.</li>
+            <li>Do not add a new top-level section; control flow belongs in Workflow.</li>
+            <li>Read both paths aloud: README exists; README does not exist.</li>
+            <li>Confirm every path terminates in a visible outcome.</li>
+          </ol>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: burying branches inside a paragraph</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> a step contains three “if” clauses, two exceptions, and a repeated operation without a clear end. Neither teammate nor agent can scan the paths.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> give each condition its own bullet, use uppercase STOP for true termination, and delimit repeated bodies with a descriptive XML-style tag such as <code>&lt;image-loop&gt;</code>. The tags are not magic syntax; they are high-visibility boundaries.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice — make failure observable</p>
+          <h3>Control flow is also a review surface</h3>
+          <p>A branch should say not only what the agent cannot do but what it reports. The course’s missing-plan guard is strong because it stops immediately and asks for the exact missing value. Whether the prompt is a command or a skill, explicit prerequisites keep the agent from silently converting absence into invention.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval — locate the capability</p>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Where does Level-3 control flow live?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Inside the Workflow section.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What exact condition makes <code>build.md</code> Level 3?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Line 17 stops immediately and asks the user when no <code>PATH_TO_PLAN</code> is supplied.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why is <code>edit_image.md</code> still a valid Level-3 prompt without a Report?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Its level is determined by loops and conditions in Workflow. Report is optional when no distinct response contract is needed.</p></details></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p>
+          <div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Use <code>build.md</code> as your proof:</strong> explain how a single early-return line promotes a workflow to Level 3, why that line belongs in Workflow, and how the same file can also exhibit Level 5.</p></div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="level-four">
+        <span class="section-number">05</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Level 4 manages information between agents</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Delegation is not merely asking for more work. The primary agent must design self-contained prompts, assign bounded slices, launch agents, collect results, and synthesize them.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — stateless workers</p>
+          <h3>“Look into this” is not a delegation plan</h3>
+          <p>A sub-agent does not inherit the primary agent’s unspoken understanding. If its prompt omits the target, evidence, constraints, or required output, it begins from a partial world. Multiplying that prompt multiplies partial worlds. The value of parallelism arrives only after the information flow is engineered.</p>
+          <p>Level 4 therefore changes the role of the primary agent. It becomes the prompt engineer for its sub-agents: it writes the prompts that other agents will execute.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — fan out, then synthesize</p>
+          <h3>One request becomes several self-contained contracts</h3>
+          <div class="workflow" aria-label="Primary agent designs sub-agent prompts, launches them in parallel, then synthesizes results">
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Request</strong><code>PROMPT_REQUEST</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Decompose</strong><code>COUNT slices</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Write prompts</strong><code>self-contained</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Launch batch</strong><code>Task × N</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Synthesize</strong><code>one report</code></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story — two delegation shapes</p>
+          <h3>Sub-agents in a batch; a full agent in the background</h3>
+          <div class="frame-sequence">
+            <figure class="frame-card">
+              <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t1935-l4-load-ai-docs-scrapers.jpg" alt="Editor showing load_ai_docs.md looping URLs and calling the docs-scraper sub-agent" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">32:15</span><code>load_ai_docs.md</code> delegates one URL at a time to <code>@agent-docs-scraper</code>. The visible loop and agent call make both control flow and delegation inspectable.</figcaption>
+            </figure>
+            <figure class="frame-card">
+              <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t2020-l4-background-append-system-prompt.jpg" alt="Editor showing background.md metadata and purpose for launching a background Claude Code instance" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">33:40</span><code>background.md</code> launches a full background Claude Code instance and appends to its system prompt. The pinned model is period detail; the durable lesson is that another agent becomes a callable component.</figcaption>
+            </figure>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p>
+          <h3>Level 4: Delegate Prompt · S usefulness / A skill</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Capability added</p><p>A <strong>Delegate Prompt</strong> launches other agents and deliberately controls what each one receives and returns. It is the D—Delegate—of the R&amp;D context-engineering framework referenced from AH-1.</p></aside>
+          <p>The S usefulness grade reflects leverage: multiple agents can inspect different slices or propose diverse solutions. The A skill grade reflects coordination: prompts must be self-contained, boundaries must reduce collision, and outputs must be comparable enough to synthesize.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — audit commands with three agents</p>
+          <h3>Read <code>parallel_subagents.md</code> as a prompt factory</h3>
+          <div class="prompt-snippet"><pre><code>PROMPT_REQUEST: $1
+COUNT: $2
+
+Workflow:
+1. Understand the requested audit.
+2. Design COUNT detailed, self-contained prompts.
+3. Launch all sub-agents in one Task batch.
+4. Collect their independent reports.
+5. Synthesize agreements, conflicts, and gaps.</code></pre><p class="prompt-snippet__note">The primary agent does not forward one vague sentence three times. It authors three executable contracts whose scope and output requirements can stand alone.</p></div>
+          <p>Because model runs are non-deterministic, three agents can return different observations even against the same files. Diversity can be useful, but it increases the need for a synthesis step that distinguishes corroboration from disagreement. Fan-out without fan-in is unfinished delegation.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — write one sub-agent contract</p>
+          <h3>Make the hidden context explicit</h3>
+          <p>Draft a sub-agent prompt for auditing only <code>.claude/commands/build.md</code>. It must include:</p>
+          <ul>
+            <li>the exact file and the course repository boundary;</li>
+            <li>the classification question: which levels are present and what lines prove each;</li>
+            <li>a prohibition on editing;</li>
+            <li>the required output: level, capability, line evidence, and one ambiguity;</li>
+            <li>a concise completion condition.</li>
+          </ul>
+          <p>Now remove the shared conversation from your mind. Could another agent execute only the text you wrote?</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: parallelizing coupled mutation</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> several sub-agents edit overlapping files or depend on context that only another sub-agent will discover. Results conflict, overwrite, or arrive in an order the plan did not model.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> parallelize independent read-only slices first. For mutation, give agents disjoint file or worktree boundaries, state dependencies, and decide where synthesis or integration occurs. The lesson’s <code>parallel_subagents.md</code> demonstrates one-batch fan-out; later orchestration material expands the isolation model.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice — preserve the concept, harden the runtime</p>
+          <h3>Background agents now have richer machinery</h3>
+          <p>The course’s shell command is an intentionally legible delegation specimen. Current practice adds structured outputs, session continuation, permission modes, typed hooks, background task management, and worktree isolation. Those features do not replace the Level-4 idea. They make its information and safety contracts more explicit.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval — follow the information</p>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What new job does the primary agent perform at Level 4?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It becomes the prompt engineer for its sub-agents: decomposing work, writing self-contained prompts, launching them, and synthesizing results.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why is delegation S-useful but A-skill?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It scales and diversifies compute, but the author must manage context, independence, output contracts, collisions, and synthesis across agent boundaries.</p></details></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p>
+          <div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Teach fan-out as information design:</strong> explain why three agents with vague prompts are not a stronger system, then reconstruct request → decomposition → self-contained prompts → one batch → synthesis without notes.</p></div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="levels-five-six">
+        <span class="section-number">06</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Level 5 consumes prompts; Level 6 produces them</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The meta-level distinction is directional. A Higher Order Prompt receives a reusable plan or prompt-like file. A Template Metaprompt emits a new prompt whose structure is constrained by a specified format.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — the direction test</p>
+          <h3>Which side of the prompt is another prompt on?</h3>
+          <p>When people first meet Levels 5 and 6, both sound like “prompts about prompts.” That phrase is too blurry to classify anything. Ask a more mechanical question: does another prompt-like artifact enter the workflow as input, or does a new prompt emerge as output?</p>
+          <p>Level 5 stabilizes a wrapper while its plan changes. Level 6 stabilizes a template while the generated prompt changes. The two patterns solve different reuse problems.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — opposite directions</p>
+          <h3>Consume versus generate</h3>
+          <div class="comparison">
+            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Level 5 · Higher Order</h3><p>A plan or bundle enters a stable prompt.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><div class="workflow workflow--stacked"><div class="workflow__step"><code>specs/thing.md</code> enters</div><div class="workflow__step"><code>build.md</code> stays stable</div><div class="workflow__step">implementation exits</div></div></div></article>
+            <article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Level 6 · Template Meta</h3><p>A high-level idea enters; a structured prompt exits.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><div class="workflow workflow--stacked"><div class="workflow__step">prompt idea enters</div><div class="workflow__step">Specified Format stays stable</div><div class="workflow__step"><code>.claude/commands/new.md</code> exits</div></div></div></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story — one frame, one live correction</p>
+          <h3>The metaprompt shows its template as a first-class section</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t2280-l6-metaprompt-specified-format.jpg" alt="Editor showing t_metaprompt_workflow.md with Documentation and Specified Format sections" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">38:00</span><code>t_metaprompt_workflow.md</code> combines <code>HIGH_LEVEL_PROMPT: $ARGUMENTS</code>, a Documentation map, and a Specified Format the agent must fill. The recording still says “follow the Instructions”; Dan corrects it live to “Workflow,” and the shipped file contains the correction.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p>The capture discrepancy is worth keeping because it models prompt maintenance. The interface is stable enough that a mistaken section reference can be identified and corrected. The file on disk—not the stale on-screen phrase—is the operational artifact.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p>
+          <h3>Level 5: Higher Order · B/A · Level 6: Template Metaprompt · S/S</h3>
+          <div class="prompt-scope">
+            <article><strong>Higher Order Prompt</strong><p>Accepts a reusable prompt, plan, or context bundle as a variable. Its top-level procedure stays consistent while the supplied artifact changes.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>Template Metaprompt</strong><p>Uses a Template or Specified Format to create a new prompt in the team’s house style. It is the “prompt that builds prompts.”</p></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — stable wrappers on both sides</p>
+          <h3><code>/build</code> consumes; <code>/t_metaprompt_workflow</code> generates</h3>
+          <p>Run <code>/build specs/thing.md</code>. The build command does not need the planning logic embedded inside itself. It validates <code>PATH_TO_PLAN</code>, reads whichever plan was supplied, implements that plan, and reports the diff. Tomorrow a different plan enters the same wrapper. That is higher-order reuse.</p>
+          <p>Now run the template metaprompt with a high-level idea: “a prompt that lists every TODO comment in the codebase and writes them to <code>todos.md</code>.” The metaprompt researches its supplied Documentation, follows its Workflow, and emits a command with the specified metadata, title, purpose, optional Variables, Workflow, and Report shape. The output is itself a reusable prompt. That is metaprompting.</p>
+          <div class="decision-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Level 5</th><th>Level 6</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What varies?</td><td>The plan/prompt-like input.</td><td>The high-level idea and generated prompt.</td></tr><tr><td>What stays stable?</td><td>The execution wrapper.</td><td>The Specified Format.</td></tr><tr><td>Course example</td><td><code>build.md</code>, <code>load_bundle.md</code></td><td><code>t_metaprompt_workflow.md</code>, <code>plan_vite_vue.md</code></td></tr><tr><td>Proof</td><td>A file path enters through Variables.</td><td>A new prompt is written to the command directory.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — classify by direction</p>
+          <h3>Answer without using the level names first</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li><code>load_bundle.md</code> receives a saved context bundle and reconstructs context. Say: “prompt-like file enters.” Then name Level 5.</li>
+            <li><code>plan_vite_vue.md</code> contains a large Plan Format with conditional blocks. Say: “structured plan exits.” Then name Level 6.</li>
+            <li>A command reads a task spec and implements it. Classify the direction before the number.</li>
+            <li>A command accepts a sentence and writes a new reusable slash command. Classify the direction before the number.</li>
+          </ol>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: free-form generation masquerading as a metaprompt</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> “Write me a good prompt” produces a different architecture every run, with renamed sections, missing Workflow, and no output location.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> encode the house style in a Template. Use placeholders for content that changes, state which sections are optional, prohibit unrequested extra sections, and give the Workflow a completion condition. Level 6 earns S skill because the template must be general enough to generate many prompts while remaining strict enough to preserve the interface.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice — metaprompt into a skill</p>
+          <h3>Generation scales when the format becomes distributable</h3>
+          <p>A template metaprompt can generate slash commands, skill bodies, or other reusable prompt artifacts. The packaging target may change, but the Level-6 move remains: encode your standards once, then make the agent instantiate them. Review generated artifacts before trusting the generator at scale.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval — point the arrow</p>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Explain Level 5 versus Level 6 in one sentence.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Level 5 consumes another prompt, plan, or prompt-like file; Level 6 generates a new prompt in a specified format.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why is <code>## Template</code> S usefulness / S skill?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It can encode the exact structure of many future artifacts, creating high leverage, but designing a reusable format that is strict, general, and unambiguous requires expert judgment.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Which version of the t_metaprompt wording is operational?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The shipped file’s corrected reference to Workflow. The frame preserves the pre-correction recording state.</p></details></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p>
+          <div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Use two arrows:</strong> draw plan → <code>build.md</code> → implementation, then idea → template metaprompt → new command. Explain what is stable and what varies in each picture.</p></div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="level-seven">
+        <span class="section-number">07</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Level 7 changes knowledge without changing procedure</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The self-improving pattern is deliberately narrow: a stable Workflow uses an Expertise section whose domain knowledge can be updated by a dedicated improve prompt.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — the expert that forgets</p>
+          <h3>A reusable workflow can stay correct while its domain knowledge ages</h3>
+          <p>Suppose a planning expert always follows the right sequence: inspect the request, consult hook conventions, identify relevant files, create a plan, and report it. The procedure remains useful. But the hook file structure, event model, or security knowledge embedded in the prompt changes as the team learns. Rewriting the entire prompt to add one lesson risks damaging the stable procedure.</p>
+          <p>Level 7 introduces a seam between <em>how the expert works</em> and <em>what the expert currently knows</em>.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — stable outside, living inside</p>
+          <h3>One prompt, two rates of change</h3>
+          <div class="prompt-scope">
+            <article><strong>Stable Workflow</strong><p>The ordered planning or building procedure changes only through deliberate prompt design. It is the expert’s operating method.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>Living Expertise</strong><p>Domain facts, conventions, and lessons are updated as the codebase and the team’s understanding evolve.</p></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story — the hook expert triplet</p>
+          <h3>Plan, build, improve</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t2565-l7-expert-expertise-section.jpg" alt="Editor showing cc_hook_expert_plan.md with an expanded Expertise section describing Claude Code hook file structure" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">42:45</span>The expanded <code>## Expertise</code> section makes “self-improving” concrete: it is a substantial block of hook-domain knowledge that a separate improve prompt can revise while leaving the planning Workflow intact.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p>The course uses three files. <code>cc_hook_expert_plan.md</code> plans with the embedded expertise. <code>cc_hook_expert_build.md</code> implements with it. <code>cc_hook_expert_improve.md</code> reviews recent hook changes and updates only the Expertise sections in the other two. The improve prompt is not permitted to redesign their workflows.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p>
+          <h3>Level 7: Self-Improving Prompt · A usefulness / S skill</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Capability added</p><p>A <strong>Self-Improving Prompt</strong> updates context inside its reusable prompt system over time. In the course pattern, a dedicated improve prompt changes only <code>## Expertise</code>; it does not mutate the expert’s Workflow.</p></aside>
+          <p>The A usefulness grade is intentionally below the Template Metaprompt’s S. Level 7 is not universally valuable. It needs a real, narrow domain where accumulated knowledge improves future work. The S skill grade reflects the difficulty of choosing trustworthy evidence, preventing drift, and keeping stable and dynamic regions separate.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — route a new lesson</p>
+          <h3>Where should “hooks now live under this directory” go?</h3>
+          <div class="decision-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Candidate change</th><th>Destination</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>New hook directory convention</td><td>Expertise</td><td>It is domain knowledge the expert consults.</td></tr><tr><td>Plan must still inspect requirements before files</td><td>Workflow</td><td>It is stable procedure, not a learned fact.</td></tr><tr><td>A recent change exposed a security caveat</td><td>Expertise</td><td>It enriches domain context for future decisions.</td></tr><tr><td>Improve prompt should now edit both Expertise and Workflow</td><td>Reject</td><td>It violates the pattern’s safety boundary.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
+          <p>The distinction protects reviewability. A reviewer can compare Expertise changes as knowledge changes. If the same process also rewrites Workflow, a learned fact can silently alter how future work is performed.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — mark the boundary</p>
+          <h3>Trace the real improve prompt read-only</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Open the plan, build, and improve files under <code>.claude/commands/experts/cc_hook_expert/</code>.</li>
+            <li>Highlight every instruction in <code>cc_hook_expert_improve.md</code> that limits edits to Expertise.</li>
+            <li>Collapse the plan and build files to their headings.</li>
+            <li>Write two columns: stable Workflow; updateable Expertise.</li>
+            <li>Describe one failure that would occur if improve could rewrite both.</li>
+          </ol>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: self-modification without a domain or boundary</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> an “improve” command rewrites wording, format, workflow, and expertise after every run, based on whatever happened most recently. The prompt drifts and no one can say which behavior is stable.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> use Level 7 only for a narrow domain with accumulating evidence. Give the improve step an explicit target section, evidence source, and prohibition against changing Workflow. If you cannot identify the knowledge that should compound, stay at a lower level.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice — knowledge has several homes</p>
+          <h3>Do not confuse a durable concept with one storage pattern</h3>
+          <p>Skills, memory files, and other knowledge assets may now carry reusable expertise. The course’s Expertise-section loop remains a clear teaching pattern because its update boundary is visible in one prompt system. Whatever the storage mechanism, separate learned domain context from the stable procedure that consumes it.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval — protect the invariant</p>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What must <code>cc_hook_expert_improve.md</code> never touch?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The Workflows of the plan and build experts. It updates only their Expertise sections.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why is Level 7 rare rather than the automatic destination?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It needs a narrow domain with useful accumulating knowledge and a trustworthy update boundary. Many prompts have no genuine self-improvement use case.</p></details></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p>
+          <div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Resolve the apparent paradox:</strong> explain how a prompt can “improve itself” while its Workflow remains stable. Use plan → build → improve and name exactly what changes.</p></div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="tier-list">
+        <span class="section-number">08</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">The tier list is a choice map</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Usefulness runs from S to D vertically; required skill runs from D to S horizontally. The point is not to memorize prestige. It is to see which capabilities return the most value for their operating cost.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — the bargain hiding in plain sight</p>
+          <h3>The most useful everyday section is not an expert skill</h3>
+          <p>The grid’s most actionable result is <code>## Workflow</code> at S usefulness and C skill. It sits near the top-left: enormous value, approachable cost. The most technically demanding cell, S/S, belongs to <code>## Template</code> and the Level-6 Template Metaprompt. That is peak leverage, but it is not the place to begin every task.</p>
+          <p>A good choice map resists two errors at once: staying forever at Level 1 because it is easy, and reaching for Level 7 because it sounds advanced. The grid makes trade-offs visible.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — a crisp redraw</p>
+          <h3>All thirteen sections and seven formats in their verified cells</h3>
+          <figure class="tier-figure">
+            <div class="tier-grid" role="img" aria-label="Tier grid of prompt sections and formats with usefulness S through D vertically and skill D through S horizontally">
+              <div class="tier-cell tier-cell--axis">Useful ↓<br>Skill →</div>
+              <div class="tier-cell tier-cell--axis">D<br>Beginner</div><div class="tier-cell tier-cell--axis">C</div><div class="tier-cell tier-cell--axis">B</div><div class="tier-cell tier-cell--axis">A</div><div class="tier-cell tier-cell--axis">S<br>Expert</div>
+
+              <div class="tier-cell tier-cell--axis tier-cell--row">S · Highest</div>
+              <div class="tier-cell"></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell" style="--cell-color:var(--ok-wash)"><span class="tier-pill">## Workflow</span></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell"></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell" style="--cell-color:var(--accent-wash)"><span class="tier-pill tier-pill--format">delegate_prompt.md · L4</span></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell" style="--cell-color:var(--accent-wash)"><span class="tier-pill">## Template</span><span class="tier-pill tier-pill--format">template_metaprompt.md · L6</span></div>
+
+              <div class="tier-cell tier-cell--axis tier-cell--row">A</div>
+              <div class="tier-cell"></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell" style="--cell-color:var(--ok-wash)"><span class="tier-pill tier-pill--format">workflow_prompt.md · L2</span></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell" style="--cell-color:var(--ok-wash)"><span class="tier-pill">## Variables</span><span class="tier-pill">## Examples</span><span class="tier-pill tier-pill--format">control_flow_prompt.md · L3</span></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell" style="--cell-color:var(--ok-wash)"><span class="tier-pill">## Expertise</span></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell" style="--cell-color:var(--ok-wash)"><span class="tier-pill tier-pill--format">self_improving_prompt.md · L7</span></div>
+
+              <div class="tier-cell tier-cell--axis tier-cell--row">B</div>
+              <div class="tier-cell"><span class="tier-pill">## High Level Prompt</span><span class="tier-pill">## Purpose</span><span class="tier-pill tier-pill--format">high_level_prompt.md · L1</span></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell"><span class="tier-pill">## Report</span><span class="tier-pill">## Instructions</span></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell"></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell"><span class="tier-pill tier-pill--format">higher_order_prompt.md · L5</span></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell"></div>
+
+              <div class="tier-cell tier-cell--axis tier-cell--row">C</div>
+              <div class="tier-cell"><span class="tier-pill"># Title</span></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell"><span class="tier-pill">## Metadata</span><span class="tier-pill">## Codebase Structure</span><span class="tier-pill">## Relevant Files</span></div>
+              <div class="tier-cell"></div><div class="tier-cell"></div><div class="tier-cell"></div>
+
+              <div class="tier-cell tier-cell--axis tier-cell--row">D · Lowest</div>
+              <div class="tier-cell"></div><div class="tier-cell"></div><div class="tier-cell"></div><div class="tier-cell"></div><div class="tier-cell"></div>
+            </div>
+            <figcaption>Designed redraw of the lesson’s final grid. Section pills are plain; format pills have accent borders. Empty cells are informative: the lesson places no entity there.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story — the ground-truth frame</p>
+          <h3>The app’s final state confirms the redraw</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t2740-final-tier-list.jpg" alt="Completed Agentic Prompt Tier List application with all sections and formats placed on a five-by-five grid" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">45:40</span>The completed on-screen grid is the grading source. The app pool contains thirteen sections and seven formats. Older references to twelve sections predate the High Level Prompt section being split into its own entity.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p>The frame matters because spoken grading unfolds over many minutes. A position can be misremembered when a section and its enclosing format have different grades. The final state reconciles all twenty entities in one picture.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p>
+          <h3>Usefulness / skill, not good / bad</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">How to read a cell</p><p><strong>S/C</strong> means S-tier usefulness and C-tier required skill. <strong>B/A</strong> means moderate usefulness and advanced skill. Low usefulness does not make a section invalid; it means its value is more situational.</p></aside>
+          <p>Title is C/D: easy and modestly useful. Metadata, Codebase Structure, and Relevant Files are C/C: useful in the right prompt but not the core engine. Variables and Examples are A/B. Expertise is A/A. Template occupies S/S because a well-designed format can generate consistent future prompts.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — choose the cheapest sufficient format</p>
+          <h3>Three tasks, three different ceilings</h3>
+          <div class="choice-grid">
+            <article><strong>Repeatable summary</strong><p>Need a named request and ordered repository observations. Choose Level 2. A Template or Expertise block adds no required capability.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>Plan-dependent build</strong><p>Need a missing-input guard and a changing plan file. <code>build.md</code> legitimately stacks Levels 2, 3, and 5.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>Parallel docs audit</strong><p>Need several independent readers and a synthesis. Choose Level 4, and pay the information-flow cost because delegation is the required capability.</p></article>
+          </div>
+          <p>The decision rule is not “always use the top-left.” It is “start with the high-value basics, then add only the capability the task cannot do without.” The grid is a prompt architecture budget.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — reconstruct the twenty cells</p>
+          <h3>Reset the app before grading yourself</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Open the tier-list app and click the pool’s × reset. The grid persists in local storage, so reload alone may preserve an old answer.</li>
+            <li>Place all thirteen sections without looking at the redraw.</li>
+            <li>Place all seven formats.</li>
+            <li>Circle Workflow S/C and Template S/S.</li>
+            <li>Compare the full grid, not only the headline cells.</li>
+            <li>For each miss, write whether you confused a section with a format, usefulness with skill, or a general capability with a situational one.</li>
+          </ol>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: “higher level” means “higher usefulness”</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> Level 7 is assumed to beat Level 4 because seven is larger than four. But the grid gives Delegate S usefulness and Self-Improving A usefulness.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> separate capability accumulation from task frequency. A self-improving domain expert is harder and rarer. Delegation applies to far more work. The number tells you which capabilities stack; the cell tells you the usefulness/skill trade-off.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice — the grid is a reasoning tool</p>
+          <h3>Do not freeze implementation details into doctrine</h3>
+          <p>Models, command packaging, and delegation runtimes change. The grid remains valuable when used to reason about architectural cost: explicit workflow is cheap and powerful; template generation is powerful and demanding; self-updating knowledge needs a real domain. Re-grade implementation details as tooling evolves, but keep the capability distinctions crisp.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval — key cells</p>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What sits in S usefulness / C skill?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The <code>## Workflow</code> section.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What two entities sit in S/S?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The <code>## Template</code> section and <code>template_metaprompt.md</code>, the Level-6 format.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Which format is S usefulness / A skill?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The Level-4 Delegate Prompt.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">How many entities are in the app pool?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Twenty: thirteen sections and seven formats.</p></details></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p>
+          <div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Defend a Level-3/4 default:</strong> use the grid to explain why a great Workflow plus visible control flow, and delegation only when needed, covers most work. Then explain why Template remains worth learning despite not being the everyday default.</p></div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="scope">
+        <span class="section-number">09</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">System prompts play a different game</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Every grade in the tier list applies to user prompts. System prompts have broader scope and longer persistence, so their errors scale across every user prompt in the conversation.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — one bad instruction, every job</p>
+          <h3>A narrow user prompt can be wrong once; a system prompt can be wrong repeatedly</h3>
+          <p>A slash command enters the context as a user prompt for a particular task. Its Variables and Report can be specific because the job is specific. A system prompt sets rules and identity for all conversations handled by that agent. It cannot be rewritten midway through a conversation, must survive scenarios its author did not enumerate, and influences every user prompt that follows.</p>
+          <p>That makes scope and persistence the controlling design variables. A system prompt is written once and run many times. Over-constraining it can make every future task brittle.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — widening consequence</p>
+          <h3>Task-local on the left; conversation-wide on the right</h3>
+          <div class="scope-axis" role="img" aria-label="Continuum from one task user prompt to all-conversation system prompt">
+            <div class="scope-axis__line"></div>
+            <div class="scope-axis__labels"><span><strong>User prompt</strong><br>one requested job · task-specific input and report</span><span><strong>System prompt</strong><br>all conversations · persistent identity and rules</span></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story — the README changes the frame</p>
+          <h3>After seven levels, the lesson zooms out to scope</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t3030-readme-spine-system-vs-user.jpg" alt="Editor showing the Agentic Prompt Engineering README outline with seven levels, sections, and System Prompts vs User Prompts" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">50:30</span>The README’s collapsed spine makes the transition explicit: after seven user-prompt formats and their sections, system versus user prompts becomes a separate design problem defined by scope and persistence.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p>
+          <h3>Scope and persistence</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Decision boundary</p><p>A <strong>user prompt</strong> requests a particular job inside the current context. A <strong>system prompt</strong> defines the agent’s persistent role and behavior across all jobs in the conversation. The tier-list grades are for user prompts only.</p></aside>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — section fit changes with scope</p>
+          <h3>A good task section can be a poor global rule</h3>
+          <div class="prompt-scope">
+            <article><strong>User prompt fit</strong><p>Variables name call-time inputs. Report prescribes the response. Relevant Files bounds a task. A deterministic Workflow reduces agency to increase repeatability.</p></article>
+            <article><strong>System prompt fit</strong><p>Purpose establishes identity. Instructions define broad behavior. Examples demonstrate patterns. A loose Workflow can orient without forcing every future task through one rigid sequence.</p></article>
+          </div>
+          <div class="decision-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>User prompt</th><th>Primary-agent system prompt</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Useful</td><td>Strong fit: role and identity</td></tr><tr><td>Instructions</td><td>Supporting guardrails</td><td>Strong fit: persistent behavior</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Useful demonstrations</td><td>Strong fit: behavioral patterns</td></tr><tr><td>Workflow</td><td>Often deterministic</td><td>Keep loose to preserve agency</td></tr><tr><td>Variables / Report</td><td>Task-specific strengths</td><td>Usually avoid as global structure</td></tr><tr><td>Template / Expertise / Metadata</td><td>Useful in specific formats</td><td>Generally avoid in the broad primary system prompt</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — classify two real files</p>
+          <h3>Command versus sub-agent</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Open <code>.claude/commands/prime.md</code>. Identify why it is a user prompt: it requests one bounded priming job.</li>
+            <li>Open <code>.claude/agents/docs-scraper.md</code>. Treat the sub-agent definition as its system prompt.</li>
+            <li>List the scraper’s Purpose, static Variables, Workflow, and Report/Response behavior.</li>
+            <li>Ask which of those sections are safe because the sub-agent itself has a narrow role.</li>
+            <li>Explain why copying that rigid structure into a general-purpose primary agent would be more consequential.</li>
+          </ol>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: applying the user-prompt tier list globally</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> because Workflow is S-useful for task prompts, a primary-agent system prompt forces every possible request through the same detailed steps.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> re-evaluate by scope. Use Purpose, broad Instructions, and Examples to shape behavior. If a Workflow is present, keep it loose enough for many scenarios. Move deterministic task plays back into user prompts where they can be selected when relevant.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice — append and overwrite are architectural choices</p>
+          <h3>Most engineers should touch system prompts sparingly</h3>
+          <p>The course’s <code>background.md</code> appends system instructions when launching a background agent. The next AH lesson uses the SDK to build specialized agents and control the system prompt more directly. Append preserves the base; overwrite assumes responsibility for the whole agent contract. Both are much broader interventions than invoking a slash command.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval — scope before syntax</p>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">The tier-list grades apply to which prompt kind?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>User prompts, including the lesson’s slash commands.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name three strong system-prompt sections and three to avoid in a broad primary agent.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Strong: Purpose, Instructions, Examples, plus at most a loose Workflow. Avoid as general defaults: Variables, Report, Expertise/Template, Metadata, Relevant Files, and Codebase Structure.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why can a system-prompt mistake be more consequential?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Its rules persist across every user prompt in the conversation rather than one bounded job.</p></details></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p>
+          <div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Explain the sentence “a bad system prompt is bad instructions for the whole job.”</strong> Contrast <code>prime.md</code> with <code>docs-scraper.md</code>, then state why a deterministic Workflow should usually remain task-local.</p></div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="current-practice">
+        <span class="section-number">10</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Current practice: keep the levels, update the container</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The lesson’s capability model has aged better than some of its implementation details. Preserve the seven-level vocabulary while treating command packaging, model IDs, and orchestration machinery as replaceable.</p>
+
+        <div class="practice-grid">
+          <article>
+            <strong>From slash command to Agent Skill</strong>
+            <p>The course stores reusable prompts under <code>.claude/commands/</code>. Agent Skills provide a newer package for reusable engineering with a <code>SKILL.md</code> entry point and progressively loaded resources. A Level-2 through Level-4 workflow still needs the same sections, control flow, and delegation contract inside that package.</p>
+            <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X2ciJedw2vU">Watch: RAW Agentic Coding: ZERO to Agent SKILL →</a>
+          </article>
+          <article>
+            <strong>From background shell command to custom agent</strong>
+            <p><code>background.md</code> is the clearest course specimen of treating an agent as callable compute. Production systems add structured output, sessions, explicit permission modes, typed hooks, and specialized system prompts. AH-3 carries this forward into domain-specific agents.</p>
+            <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6wR6xblSays">Watch: Agentic Coding ENDGAME: Build your Claude Code SDK Custom Agents →</a>
+          </article>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="current-practice">
+          <p class="current-practice__label">What has not changed</p>
+          <p>Prompt quality still compounds; the trifecta still needs a readable interface; Workflow remains the high-value core; explicit control flow prevents guessing; delegation still requires self-contained context; Template still converts house style into generation; self-improvement still needs a narrow knowledge boundary; and system prompts still carry wider scope and persistence.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <aside class="callout callout--warning">
+          <p class="callout__label">Period details in the course</p>
+          <p><code>claude-opus-4-1-20250805</code>, Firecrawl MCP, the documentation TTL, and the exact background CLI flags belong to the recorded implementation. Do not “correct” the lesson frames. When running the lab, change a model or dependency only if the local tool rejects it, and record that as environment drift rather than a prompt-level concept.</p>
+        </aside>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section chapter-section--wide" id="lab">
+        <span class="section-number">11</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Graded lab · Build the map from the course code</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">This lab stays entirely inside <code>agentic-horizon/agentic-prompt-engineering/</code>. You will operate the lesson’s app and prompt library, trigger observable branches, and produce evidence that another reviewer can grade.</p>
+
+        <aside class="callout callout--warning">
+          <p class="callout__label">Scope and safety</p>
+          <p>Work in a disposable copy or branch of the course repository. Do not run <code>create_image.md</code>, <code>edit_image.md</code>, or <code>load_ai_docs.md</code> unless their external services are already configured; they are read-and-analyze specimens here. The core pass path requires no image or scraping service.</p>
+        </aside>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-setup">
+          <span class="graded-badge">Checkpoint 0 · 10 points</span>
