116:        <p class="source-note"><strong>Source discipline.</strong> This chapter treats the shipped lesson code as the operational source and the final on-screen grid as the grading source. The app contains thirteen section entities in <code>apps/prompt_tier_list/src/constants/entities.ts</code>; older prose that says twelve is documentation drift. The lesson’s model IDs and specific MCP dependencies are preserved as period details, not silently modernized.</p>
250:            <img src="../media/frames/ah-2-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats/t0765-quick-plan-collapsed-sections.jpg" alt="Editor showing quick-plan.md collapsed to Variables, Instructions, Workflow, and Report headings" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
253:          <p>This view is a maintenance tool. You can compare two prompts by their headings before comparing prose. You can notice that one prompt has a Report and another intentionally does not. You can find a Variables block without searching for every <code>$1</code>. You can review whether knowledge accidentally leaked into the Workflow or whether execution steps are hiding inside Instructions.</p>
267:            <article><strong>Variables</strong><p><code>USER_PROMPT: $ARGUMENTS</code> changes each run. <code>PLAN_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY: `specs/`</code> is a fixed value referenced throughout.</p></article>
289:            <article><strong>Workflow ≠ Instructions</strong><p><strong>Fail:</strong> put the main play in an undifferentiated list of advice. <strong>Fix:</strong> Workflow is the numbered sequence the agent executes; Instructions are auxiliary rules around those steps and can often be nested into the relevant step.</p></article>
305:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is the difference between Workflow and Instructions?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Workflow is the ordered executable play. Instructions are auxiliary guardrails that support the play and can often live under the step they constrain.</p></details></div>
367:          <p>A Level-1 prompt generally uses one to three sections: Title, the required High Level Prompt, perhaps Purpose. A Level-2 prompt requires Workflow and can add Variables, Instructions, Report, Metadata, Relevant Files, or Codebase Structure as its job demands. The format grade of Level 2 is A/C; its defining <code>## Workflow</code> section is S/C.</p>
376:USER_PROMPT: $ARGUMENTS
377:PLAN_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY: specs/
381:- Understand USER_PROMPT.
382:- Create the plan in PLAN_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY.
387:            <p class="prompt-snippet__note"><strong>Dynamic:</strong> <code>USER_PROMPT</code> changes with each invocation. <strong>Static:</strong> <code>PLAN_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY</code> stays fixed until the prompt author changes it once.</p>
